\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S1}{\ignorespaces Sofular--Melchsee--Frutt (MF) correspondence across the overlapping interval. (a) Sofular $\delta ^{13}$C and published labels \citep  {held2024dansgaard}. (b) MF $\delta ^{18}$O and published labels \citep  {fohlmeister2023role}. Fine traces show observations; thick curves show Gaussian smoothing with $\sigma =50$ yr. Gray connectors mark visually inferred correspondences without tuning either chronology. MF 6.16 corresponds to an unnumbered Sofular feature near 175.2 kyr BP; Sofular 6.9 is a separate older event. Dashed leaders identify approximate label anchors, and gray shading marks the MF data gap. The Sofular proxy axis is reversed.}}{10}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:S01}{{S1}{10}{Sofular--Melchsee--Frutt (MF) correspondence across the overlapping interval. (a) Sofular $\delta ^{13}$C and published labels \citep {held2024dansgaard}. (b) MF $\delta ^{18}$O and published labels \citep {fohlmeister2023role}. Fine traces show observations; thick curves show Gaussian smoothing with $\sigma =50$ yr. Gray connectors mark visually inferred correspondences without tuning either chronology. MF 6.16 corresponds to an unnumbered Sofular feature near 175.2 kyr BP; Sofular 6.9 is a separate older event. Dashed leaders identify approximate label anchors, and gray shading marks the MF data gap. The Sofular proxy axis is reversed}{figure.1}{}}
\citation{fohlmeister2023role}
\citation{held2024dansgaard}
\citation{fohlmeister2023role}
\citation{held2024dansgaard}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S2}{\ignorespaces The 21-event MF--Sofular sequence. (a) Composite event times and source records, with the displayed division at 175 kyr BP. (b) MF $\delta ^{18}$O and events 6.1--6.16 \citep  {fohlmeister2023role}. (c) Sofular $\delta ^{13}$C, with published labels 6.9--6.13 in brackets and composite labels 6.17--6.21 \citep  {held2024dansgaard}. Gray points show observations; colored curves show smoothing with $\sigma =50$ yr. Filled circles mark maximum-gradient event times assigned near literature anchors (Text~S1). Open squares and horizontal bars show individual U--Th control ages and reported $2\sigma $ errors, rather than event-age intervals. The Sofular proxy axis is reversed.}}{11}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:S02}{{S2}{11}{The 21-event MF--Sofular sequence. (a) Composite event times and source records, with the displayed division at 175 kyr BP. (b) MF $\delta ^{18}$O and events 6.1--6.16 \citep {fohlmeister2023role}. (c) Sofular $\delta ^{13}$C, with published labels 6.9--6.13 in brackets and composite labels 6.17--6.21 \citep {held2024dansgaard}. Gray points show observations; colored curves show smoothing with $\sigma =50$ yr. Filled circles mark maximum-gradient event times assigned near literature anchors (Text~S1). Open squares and horizontal bars show individual U--Th control ages and reported $2\sigma $ errors, rather than event-age intervals. The Sofular proxy axis is reversed}{figure.2}{}}
\citation{rasmussen2014stratigraphic}
\citation{rasmussen2014stratigraphic}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S3}{\ignorespaces NGRIP event-age uncertainty. (a) Working definition-error standard deviations for 34 warming boundaries (orange circles) and 35 cooling boundaries (blue squares) \citep  {rasmussen2014stratigraphic}. The black line joins chronology envelopes: published maximum counting errors and the adopted 4.5\%-of-b2k-age model-extension envelope, both interpreted approximately as $2\sigma $ (Text~S2). (b) Combined chronology-plus-definition offsets: 30 realizations (gray), median (blue), and pointwise 2.5th--97.5th percentiles (shading) of 10,000 ordered realizations. Positive offsets indicate older ages. The dashed boundary separates counted and modeled ages at 60.152 kyr BP. These ranges describe the working uncertainty model.}}{12}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:S03}{{S3}{12}{NGRIP event-age uncertainty. (a) Working definition-error standard deviations for 34 warming boundaries (orange circles) and 35 cooling boundaries (blue squares) \citep {rasmussen2014stratigraphic}. The black line joins chronology envelopes: published maximum counting errors and the adopted 4.5\%-of-b2k-age model-extension envelope, both interpreted approximately as $2\sigma $ (Text~S2). (b) Combined chronology-plus-definition offsets: 30 realizations (gray), median (blue), and pointwise 2.5th--97.5th percentiles (shading) of 10,000 ordered realizations. Positive offsets indicate older ages. The dashed boundary separates counted and modeled ages at 60.152 kyr BP. These ranges describe the working uncertainty model}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S4}{\ignorespaces Chronology and event-timing sensitivity of the speleothem composite. (a) Age offsets from nominal event times, with nominal ages beside labels. Dots mark medians; thick and thin bars span the 16th--84th and 2.5th--97.5th percentiles of 10,000 ordered realizations. Red and purple distinguish MF and Sofular; the dashed line marks zero offset. (b) Pearson correlations among sampled age anomalies, with the white boundary separating records. The distributions reflect the shared MF chronology factor, So-4 dated-depth perturbations, and alternative timing settings described in Text~S2; they are not full age-model posterior intervals.}}{13}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:S04}{{S4}{13}{Chronology and event-timing sensitivity of the speleothem composite. (a) Age offsets from nominal event times, with nominal ages beside labels. Dots mark medians; thick and thin bars span the 16th--84th and 2.5th--97.5th percentiles of 10,000 ordered realizations. Red and purple distinguish MF and Sofular; the dashed line marks zero offset. (b) Pearson correlations among sampled age anomalies, with the white boundary separating records. The distributions reflect the shared MF chronology factor, So-4 dated-depth perturbations, and alternative timing settings described in Text~S2; they are not full age-model posterior intervals}{figure.4}{}}
\citation{barker2011800}
\citation{barker2011800}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S5}{\ignorespaces Barker SpeleoAge chronology uncertainty \citep  {barker2011800}. (a) Published combined control uncertainties (black circles), used as uniform proposal half-widths, and the auxiliary 400-kyr control (blue diamond; 1.30 kyr). The connecting line is an interpolated offset bound. (b) Twenty-five ordered offset maps (gray), median (blue), and pointwise 2.5th--97.5th percentiles (shading) of 10,000 maps. Positive offsets indicate older ages; red ticks locate the 70 events. Ochre shading marks the alignment gap, across which offsets are interpolated. The blue dotted line marks the last published control at 378.15 kyr; pale blue shading extends to the auxiliary control. Ranges describe the adopted chronology scenario (Text~S2).}}{14}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:S05}{{S5}{14}{Barker SpeleoAge chronology uncertainty \citep {barker2011800}. (a) Published combined control uncertainties (black circles), used as uniform proposal half-widths, and the auxiliary 400-kyr control (blue diamond; 1.30 kyr). The connecting line is an interpolated offset bound. (b) Twenty-five ordered offset maps (gray), median (blue), and pointwise 2.5th--97.5th percentiles (shading) of 10,000 maps. Positive offsets indicate older ages; red ticks locate the 70 events. Ochre shading marks the alignment gap, across which offsets are interpolated. The blue dotted line marks the last published control at 378.15 kyr; pale blue shading extends to the auxiliary control. Ranges describe the adopted chronology scenario (Text~S2)}{figure.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S6}{\ignorespaces Chronology sensitivity of the primary phase association. Histograms show (a) log-likelihood gain per event, $G$ (bits/event), (b) nominal likelihood-ratio $p$, (c) preferred phase, (d) maximum/minimum phase rate ratio, and (e) full-minus-reduced AIC for 9,982 supported age fits, each containing 53 response events. Eighteen source realizations fall outside observation support. Black solid and red dashed lines mark nominal-age estimates and MC medians; gray dotted lines mark $p=0.05$ and zero AIC difference. Distributions describe chronology and transition-timing sensitivity, not event-process sampling uncertainty. The annotated threshold fraction is not a null $p$ value (Text~S4).}}{15}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:S06}{{S6}{15}{Chronology sensitivity of the primary phase association. Histograms show (a) log-likelihood gain per event, $G$ (bits/event), (b) nominal likelihood-ratio $p$, (c) preferred phase, (d) maximum/minimum phase rate ratio, and (e) full-minus-reduced AIC for 9,982 supported age fits, each containing 53 response events. Eighteen source realizations fall outside observation support. Black solid and red dashed lines mark nominal-age estimates and MC medians; gray dotted lines mark $p=0.05$ and zero AIC difference. Distributions describe chronology and transition-timing sensitivity, not event-process sampling uncertainty. The annotated threshold fraction is not a null $p$ value (Text~S4)}{figure.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S7}{\ignorespaces Chronology sensitivity of the Barker varying-threshold phase association. Histograms show (a) log-likelihood gain per event, $G$ (bits/event), (b) nominal likelihood-ratio $p$, (c) preferred phase, (d) maximum/minimum phase rate ratio, and (e) full-minus-reduced AIC for 10,000 supported age fits, each containing 69 response events. Black solid and red dashed lines mark nominal-age estimates and MC medians; gray dotted lines mark $p=0.05$ and zero AIC difference. Phases are unwrapped around the nominal estimate, with labels modulo $360^\circ $. Distributions describe chronology sensitivity, not event-process sampling uncertainty. The annotated threshold fraction is not a null $p$ value (Text~S4).}}{16}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:S07}{{S7}{16}{Chronology sensitivity of the Barker varying-threshold phase association. Histograms show (a) log-likelihood gain per event, $G$ (bits/event), (b) nominal likelihood-ratio $p$, (c) preferred phase, (d) maximum/minimum phase rate ratio, and (e) full-minus-reduced AIC for 10,000 supported age fits, each containing 69 response events. Black solid and red dashed lines mark nominal-age estimates and MC medians; gray dotted lines mark $p=0.05$ and zero AIC difference. Phases are unwrapped around the nominal estimate, with labels modulo $360^\circ $. Distributions describe chronology sensitivity, not event-process sampling uncertainty. The annotated threshold fraction is not a null $p$ value (Text~S4)}{figure.7}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S8}{\ignorespaces Reduced-model bootstrap calibration for (a) the primary catalogue and (b) Barker varying-threshold events. Gray histograms show 9,999 simulated likelihood ratios per catalogue; dashed curves show the nominal $\chi ^2_2$ reference, and colored lines mark observed statistics. Simulations retain the exact conditioning events and generate younger continuous event times with immediate history updates before refitting both models (Text~S3). The plus-one bootstrap $p$ values are 0.0003 and 0.0077. Displayed binomial intervals quantify simulation uncertainty in the null exceedance probabilities. Forcing interpolants and exact response boundaries remain fixed.}}{17}{figure.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bootstrap_comparison}{{S8}{17}{Reduced-model bootstrap calibration for (a) the primary catalogue and (b) Barker varying-threshold events. Gray histograms show 9,999 simulated likelihood ratios per catalogue; dashed curves show the nominal $\chi ^2_2$ reference, and colored lines mark observed statistics. Simulations retain the exact conditioning events and generate younger continuous event times with immediate history updates before refitting both models (Text~S3). The plus-one bootstrap $p$ values are 0.0003 and 0.0077. Displayed binomial intervals quantify simulation uncertainty in the null exceedance probabilities. Forcing interpolants and exact response boundaries remain fixed}{figure.8}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S9}{\ignorespaces Sensitivity to exponential-history decay and initialization on identical 165.058-kyr support with 53 response events. (a--c) Decay time $\tau =1$, 1.5, 2, 3 or 5 kyr with unobserved pre-anchor history zero. (d--f) Pre-anchor weighted history 0, 0.5 or 1 at $\tau =1.5$ kyr. Columns show log-likelihood gain per event, $G$ (bits/event), preferred phase, and maximum/minimum phase rate ratio. Open markers and dashed horizontal lines identify the primary settings. All scenarios retain exact conditioning events, response boundaries and nominal climate scaling, with a nonpositive history coefficient. Connected points are prespecified estimates, not uncertainty intervals (Text~S6).}}{18}{figure.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:S09}{{S9}{18}{Sensitivity to exponential-history decay and initialization on identical 165.058-kyr support with 53 response events. (a--c) Decay time $\tau =1$, 1.5, 2, 3 or 5 kyr with unobserved pre-anchor history zero. (d--f) Pre-anchor weighted history 0, 0.5 or 1 at $\tau =1.5$ kyr. Columns show log-likelihood gain per event, $G$ (bits/event), preferred phase, and maximum/minimum phase rate ratio. Open markers and dashed horizontal lines identify the primary settings. All scenarios retain exact conditioning events, response boundaries and nominal climate scaling, with a nonpositive history coefficient. Connected points are prespecified estimates, not uncertainty intervals (Text~S6)}{figure.9}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S10}{\ignorespaces Sensitivity to background-climate shape and event history on identical 165.058-kyr support with 53 response events. Columns show log-likelihood gain per event, $G$ (bits/event), preferred phase, and maximum/minimum phase rate ratio. (a--c) Linear LR04/CO$_2$ terms supplemented by either square, both squares, or both squares and their product (``+ interaction''). Exponential history is retained. (d--f) Exponential history, a rectangular 1.5-kyr count, elapsed time $w$ since the last event, and $\log (1+w/1\ {\rm  kyr})$. Linear climate terms are retained. Points show nominal-age estimates without uncertainty intervals; dashed lines mark the main specification. Only the phase block differs between paired reduced/full models (Text~S6).}}{19}{figure.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:S10}{{S10}{19}{Sensitivity to background-climate shape and event history on identical 165.058-kyr support with 53 response events. Columns show log-likelihood gain per event, $G$ (bits/event), preferred phase, and maximum/minimum phase rate ratio. (a--c) Linear LR04/CO$_2$ terms supplemented by either square, both squares, or both squares and their product (``+ interaction''). Exponential history is retained. (d--f) Exponential history, a rectangular 1.5-kyr count, elapsed time $w$ since the last event, and $\log (1+w/1\ {\rm kyr})$. Linear climate terms are retained. Points show nominal-age estimates without uncertainty intervals; dashed lines mark the main specification. Only the phase block differs between paired reduced/full models (Text~S6)}{figure.10}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S11}{\ignorespaces Exploratory LR04 modulation for the primary catalogue (a,b) and Barker varying-threshold catalogue (c,d). (a,c) Phase multipliers from one continuous interaction model evaluated at exposure-weighted LR04 quartiles. Dots mark maxima; shading spans pointwise 2.5th--97.5th chronology percentiles. Unity denotes zero phase contribution, and Minimum/Maximum labels identify precession-index extrema. (b,d) Log-likelihood gain per event, $G$ (bits/event), from adding interaction terms; solid and dashed lines mark nominal estimates and MC medians, and pale bands span central 95\% chronology ranges. There are 499 valid primary and 500 Barker age fits. Nominal interaction $p$ values are 0.187 and 0.0775; chronology ranges do not include full sampling uncertainty (Text~S7).}}{20}{figure.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:climate_phase_comparison}{{S11}{20}{Exploratory LR04 modulation for the primary catalogue (a,b) and Barker varying-threshold catalogue (c,d). (a,c) Phase multipliers from one continuous interaction model evaluated at exposure-weighted LR04 quartiles. Dots mark maxima; shading spans pointwise 2.5th--97.5th chronology percentiles. Unity denotes zero phase contribution, and Minimum/Maximum labels identify precession-index extrema. (b,d) Log-likelihood gain per event, $G$ (bits/event), from adding interaction terms; solid and dashed lines mark nominal estimates and MC medians, and pale bands span central 95\% chronology ranges. There are 499 valid primary and 500 Barker age fits. Nominal interaction $p$ values are 0.187 and 0.0775; chronology ranges do not include full sampling uncertainty (Text~S7)}{figure.11}{}}
